<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WPR.Input.AndroidSensor - the device's real accelerometer, read from android.hardware.SensorManager.

    Fills WPR.Engine.Sensors.IAccelerometerProvider and nothing else. This is the shape a module is
    supposed to have: one seam, one technology, one project reference - and since 2026-09-05, no
    package references at all.

    Named for the platform API it drives, per the module convention. That convention says "name the
    technology, not the platform", and Android's sensor framework IS the technology here - the same
    reading that makes WPR.Notifications.AndroidChannel legitimate. It was WPR.Input.XamarinEssentials
    until 2026-09-05, which was an accurate name right up until the library came out.

    Xamarin.Essentials is GONE, and that is a fix rather than a tidy-up. Its OnSensorChanged read
    Android.Hardware.SensorEvent.Values three times per event - once per axis - and that property
    compiles to JavaArray<float>.FromJniHandle(..., TransferLocalRef), i.e. every call mints a
    wrapper that owns a JNI reference and must be disposed. Essentials disposed none of them, so at
    50Hz it leaked 150 JNI references a second and reads started returning a frozen zero vector
    about a thousand samples in: tilt worked for twenty seconds and then silently stopped. See
    AndroidAccelerometerProvider for the full account.

    Dropping that package also drops the reason the Xamarin.Google.Guava.ListenableFuture 1.0.0.16
    pin used to be here. Essentials 1.7.3 dragged in the 2021-era AndroidX set including Guava
    ListenableFuture 1.0.0.2; because a module restores as its own graph, the head ended up with
    both that jar and the 1.0.0.16 the head resolves, and R8 failed with "Type
    com.google.common.util.concurrent.ListenableFuture is defined multiple times". With no packages
    here there is one copy again and nothing to pin. (Do NOT reach for
    XamarinGoogleGuavaListenableFutureOptOut if a duplicate ever reappears - see the long comment in
    WPR.Platform.Android.csproj; those opt-outs SIGSEGV the launcher seconds after start.)

    net8.0-android only. Spelled as a singular <TargetFramework> deliberately -
    Src/Directory.Build.targets only strips android legs from a plural <TargetFrameworks>, so the
    singular form is what keeps an android-only project intact on a machine without the workload,
    and WPR.Windows.slnf is the mechanism that keeps it out of a desktop build.

    Lived in WPR.Platform.Android/Input/ until 2026-09-02.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-android</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>21.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <AssemblyName>WPR.Input.AndroidSensor</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>WPR.Input.AndroidSensor</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Sensors\WPR.Engine.Sensors.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
